{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["The sense of <b>And Moses saw the people</b> is to say that even though Aaron was acquitted in that he was not negligent concerning them, Moses saw in his heart concerning the people that they were exposed, for they are a nation lost to counsel with no understanding among them, in the manner of \"and you let slip all my counsel\" (Proverbs 1:25), as it says, \"for he caused turmoil in Judah and dealt treacherously with HaShem\" (II Chronicles 28:19): <b>because Aaron had let them loose</b>, removing from them all counsel and discipline, so they are like sheep scattered upon the mountains (I Kings 22:17) with no counselor or guide. And he said this because they were thinking that the calf would show them the way, but they are as though they have no counsel and do not know the way they should go or the deed they should do, for some of them intended it for good, according to their reasoning, and some for outright evil, and each one of them turns to his own way." "And he said, <b>for a shamtzah among their risers</b>, for even those whose intent was not evil would produce concerning them, for generations, a trace and an evil report. So the whole people are devoid of counsel and discipline — some of them toward evil, and some of them toward raising up slander on the lip of the tongue and the report of the nation. And he said this against Aaron, to say that all of them sinned. And this is according to the view of Onkelos (Onkelos on Exodus 32:25), who translated \"לְשִׁמְצָה בְּקָמֵיהֶם\" — \"לְאַפָּקָא שׁוֹם בִּישׁ בְּדָרֵיהוֹן\" (to bring forth an evil name in their generations). And since he said \"in their generations,\" it appears that he wanted to explain that the calf which they made would be a trace of evil in the generations of Israel, for they will say: it was not for nothing that our ancestors made the calf and worshipped it; rather, they knew of it that it was what brought them up from the land of Egypt, and they found benefit in its worship for themselves, and we too will do likewise, for it is good for us to worship it, as the matter was with Jeroboam, who said, \"It is too much for you to go up to Jerusalem; behold your gods, O Israel, which brought you up from the land of Egypt\" (I Kings 12:28). Moses saw that for generations they would transgress through it." "<b>וְשִׁמְצָה</b> has the meaning of an evil report according to the words of Onkelos (Onkelos on Exodus 32:25), and this is a term familiar in the words of our Sages: שֶׁמֶץ פְּסוּל, a trace of a defect (Pesahim 3a). And perhaps according to their view it is what Scripture says: \"and my ear took a שֵׁמֶץ from it\" (Job 4:12) — that his ear took up the blemish of man and the deficiency in him, to say even though \"the dwellers in houses of clay whose foundation is in the dust\" (ibid. vv. 18–19). And likewise, \"What שֵׁמֶץ of a matter is heard in Him\" (ibid. 26:14) — that there is in all His ways no deficiency or slander whatsoever, only praise and glorification. And according to the plain sense, שֶׁמֶץ means a small amount: \"my ear took a little from it,\" and \"what שֵׁמֶץ of a matter is heard in Him\" — that all that is heard and said concerning the power of El and His mighty acts is but a small thing compared to the greatness of His deeds. And so too <b>לְשִׁמְצָה בְּקָמֵיהֶם</b>: he says that Aaron let the people loose, to diminish them among their enemies, for this great sin will diminish them in the eyes of their enemies, or it will diminish their merit when they come to wage war against an enemy. And it is like the matter stated (Ezekiel 29:15), \"And I will diminish them, so as not to rule over the nations,\" or like the matter stated (Jeremiah 10:24), \"Correct me, HaShem, but in measure, not in Your anger, lest You diminish me.\""], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:20:49.488130247Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}